{"step_index":1088,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:30:04Z","thinking":"**Considering the radius theorem**\n\nThe goal is to prove `rad(G) + 1 ≤ roman_domination_number`. Thinking about how to approach the proof in Lean 4. There's a concern about completing the full general proof within the time constraints. A relevant theorem has been previously proven: `radius_add_one_le_card : GraphCalc.radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V` which might be useful.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -u -c '\nimport networkx as nx\n\natlas = nx.graph_atlas_g()\n\ncount = 0\nexamples = []\nfor G in atlas:\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\n    if n < 2 or not nx.is_connected(G): continue\n    rad = nx.radius(G)\n    if rad >= 2:\n        # compute roman domination number\n        import pulp\n        prob = pulp.LpProblem(\"Roman\", pulp.LpMinimize)\n        nodes = list(G.nodes())\n        x = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\"x_{v}\", cat=\"Binary\") for v in nodes}\n        y = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\"y_{v}\", cat=\"Binary\") for v in nodes}\n        prob += pulp.lpSum(x[v] + 2*y[v] for v in nodes)\n        for v in nodes:\n            prob += x[v] + y[v] + pulp.lpSum(y[u] for u in G.neighbors(v)) >= 1\n            prob += x[v] + y[v] <= 1\n        prob.solve(pulp.PULP_CBC_CMD(msg=0))\n        rom = int(pulp.value(prob.objective))\n        if rom < n:\n            count += 1\n            if len(examples) < 10:\n                examples.append((n, rad, rom, list(G.edges())))\n\nprint(f\"Total graphs with rad >= 2 and roman < n in atlas (up to n=7): {count}\")\nfor ex in examples:\n    print(f\"n={ex[0]}, rad={ex[1]}, rom={ex[2]}, edges={ex[3]}\")\n'","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Inspecting graphs with rad >= 2 and roman < n","toolSummary":"Inspect graphs with rad >= 2 and roman < n"}}]}
